														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4817617">
									

<p>Hey cybersqyd, thanks for the crit!</p>
<p>So, about the interview logs: they're going to be a little atypical. Specifically, these are not going to be interviews where the Foundation actually goes in and pulls someone aside; they'll be more like passive conversation that provides exposition about the party and the DJ groups. The Foundation will use this anomaly as an optional relaxation opportunity for approved staff. That way, they can collect information while also keeping staff morale high.</p>
<p>You make a good point about the narrative hook- the story doesn't exactly start off fast. It won't be super easy to create a way to get the reader invested right off the bat, I think. While this could (and maybe even <em>should</em>) be the type of article that the reader has to be willing to stick around for, I think that may ward off some of the attention the article would get. I'll ask around and see what kinds of conflicts would fit in this situation, and I may stick to your suggestion if need be.</p>
<p>The emotional arc of the DJs themselves will be heavily illustrated through the audio logs, which will be short and sweet (with the likely exception of the final log). This will likely develop in a way similar to that of <a href="http://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/scp-2772">SCP-2772</a>, but more simply/directly. I'll have to figure out a way to keep the reader interested through both the interviews and the audio logs, though.</p>
<p>The majority of the issue here seems to be in keeping the article engaging throughout the narrative, so I'm going to try and think of some ways to keep it engaging. If you have any ideas/suggestions other than your previous ones, please, let me know!</p>

							</div>

								
					
